{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_k32hzb7dzin4rr6zrlczc7qwww6dx42v4vymwcnhabeahtvgbrkq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Here is what I found and fixed.\n\n## Bugs found and fixed in the Rust translation\n\n**1. The crate had no C-ABI surface at all** (bin-only, no `cdylib`, no `#[no_mangle]`). I added `src/lib.rs` + `src/ffi.rs` exporting `process_strings` with the exact C signature, and refactored the translated logic to be generic over a `MemAccess` trait so *the same code* serves both the emulated `main` frame (driver binary) and real pointers (FFI) — no duplicated logic, no stubs.\n\n**2. The model of the C `main` stack frame was fabricated, and produced wrong output.** The C code calls `strcmp`/`strlen` on buffers that need not be NUL-terminated, so its result depends on the uninitialised frame bytes. The old model invented a synthetic junk pattern. Real divergence:\n\n```\ninput \"2 0 10 65×10 10 65×10\"   →  C printed 1,  Rust printed 0\n```\n\nI wrote a ptrace dumper (`probe/dump_frame.c`), captured the real frame 24×, and generated `src/frame_junk.rs` (per-byte majority vote, so the zero pattern the C branches on is exact). Cross-validated two independent ways: the C program's own observable output (2041/2046 probed offsets agree; the 5 that don't are ASLR-dependent pointer bytes) and a fresh ptrace capture asserted by a test.\n\n**3. The tail of the frame was mapped to the wrong offsets** — `flags`/`operation` were modelled at +16/+20 instead of +20/+24, and the two loop counters (+32/+40) were missing. Corrected from the disassembly and confirmed with a ptrace capture using distinctive scalars.\n\n**4. `snprintf(\"*%s*\")` emulation** allocated a `Vec` and stopped reading the pattern at truncation; glibc reads the whole string. Rewritten allocation-free and faithful.\n\n## Verification artifacts (`SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`)\n\n- **Configurations:** `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]`, `CMakeLists.txt` no options → exactly one combination, enumerated mechanically by `probe/check_features.sh`.\n- **Symbols:** `nm -D` diff between `libcstrfun.so` (from `c_src/src/lib.c`) and `libdriver.so` is **empty**.\n- **Phase B:** 46 configuration rows, all `[x]`, each with randomized fixed-seed inputs through both `.so` exports (`tests/ffi_diff.rs`, 39 tests) plus executable-level rows.\n- **Phase C:** 16 library error rows + 8 driver rows + 6 generic boundaries (NULL pointers, zero/oversized lengths, out-of-range `operation` values incl. `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX`/500 random ints), all with passing tests (`tests/ffi_errors.rs`, 20 tests). The loop-bound underflow in `match_pattern` is verified to walk off the buffer and die with the *same signal* in both, and to find a match at the *same offset* when one exists.\n- **Results:** 80 tests pass; ~2500 randomized executable-level cases and ~1300 cases with a ptrace-controlled frame — **0 real divergences**.\n\n## One documented, irreducible limit\n\nThe non-zero junk bytes are the loader's saved stack pointers; their low byte is `addr & 0xff` of a 16-byte-aligned address, so it is zero in ~1 run of 16. The C program therefore contradicts *itself* between runs on some inputs:\n\n```\n60 runs of the same input:  C → 54× \"0\", 6× \"10\"   |  Rust → 60× \"0\"\n```\n\nThe model picks the majority behaviour (best achievable). `probe/fuzz_stable.py` runs the C side 5× per input and reports **0 stable mismatches**, and `tests/exe_frame.rs` removes the lottery entirely by forcing the C program's uninitialised bytes to the modelled snapshot with `probe/inject_frame.c`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2585,"cache_read_input_tokens":342177,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2585,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"15f0aaf7-a9d4-460c-89ca-e65368d5a4d2","uuid":"51745e49-0cfd-4b84-b911-76bdc624e9c0","timestamp":"2026-08-20T01:02:52.326Z"}
